{"maxId":314,"entries":{"chapter-85-study-upper-gastrointestinal-disorders|Chapter 85 Study: Upper Gastrointestinal Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|GERD and ulcers may present with anemia, anorexia, vomiting or weight loss rather than typical pain; mild symptoms can accompany severe esophagitis. Figure 85-2 includes dysphagia among nonspecific presentations of reflux esophagitis in older adults (pp1315–1318).":["u3"],"copy|The chapter advises great caution with empiric PPI diagnosis in older adults and generally favors endoscopic evaluation rather than relying on symptom response (p1315, p1317).":["u4"],"copy|Peptic ulcer risk reflects H. pylori, mucosa-damaging drugs and impaired mucosal defenses; review NSAIDs and antithrombotic exposure (p1315).":["u5"],"copy|PPIs heal erosive esophagitis more effectively than H2 blockers. Studied daily regimens include omeprazole 20 mg and pantoprazole 40 mg (p1319).":["u6"],"copy|Reassess the indication for ongoing acid suppression; the chapter explicitly includes deprescribing when warranted (p1315). The book reports associations between PPI use and hip, wrist or spine fractures, and a meta-analysis reporting about twice the risk of C. difficile infection (pp1322–1323).":["u7"],"copy|H. pylori tests can be falsely negative during antisecretory or antibiotic treatment and with gastric atrophy; biopsy location matters (p1327).":["u8"],"copy|Confirm eradication, generally at least 1 month after treatment, using breath/stool testing or endoscopy when clinically indicated (p1327).":["u9"],"copy|The chapter describes PPI-based antibiotic combinations and notes greater eradication with 14 versus 7 days, while acknowledging no studies specifically evaluating the clinical usefulness of two-week triple therapy in older patients at the time of publication (p1328).":["u10"],"copy|UGIB originates proximal to the ligament of Treitz; it may cause hematemesis, melena, hematochezia or occult blood loss/anemia (p1331).":["u11"],"copy|Assess hemodynamics immediately and resuscitate as needed; review previous bleeding, ulcerogenic drugs, anticoagulants, blood count and coagulation studies (p1332–1333).":["u12"],"copy|The book's transfusion threshold is hemoglobin at most 8 g/dL; it also advises transfusion above that level with volume depletion or severe comorbidity (p1333).":["u13"],"copy|Pre-endoscopy omeprazole/pantoprazole 80 mg IV bolus, then 8 mg/hour, may reduce endoscopic treatment requirements but does not improve final clinical outcomes (p1333).":["u14"],"copy|After endoscopic hemostasis of high-risk ulcer stigmata, the chapter describes 72 hours of IV PPI; cited meta-analysis found oral and IV therapy similarly effective (p1333).":["u15"],"reflux-despite-acid-suppression|Reflux despite acid suppression":["u303"],"ulcer-treatment-and-bleeding-decisions|Ulcer treatment and bleeding decisions":["u304"],"when-reflux-is-severe-but-heartburn-is-modest|When reflux is severe but heartburn is modest":["u305"],"what-each-investigation-contributes|What each investigation contributes":["u306"],"selecting-and-reviewing-acid-suppression|Selecting and reviewing acid suppression":["u307"],"peptic-ulcer-disease-identify-the-lesion-and-its-cause|Peptic ulcer disease: identify the lesion and its cause":["u308"],"h-pylori-establish-active-infection-and-document-clearance|H. pylori: establish active infection and document clearance":["u309"],"acute-hemorrhage-stabilize-the-patient-then-define-the-lesion|Acute hemorrhage: stabilize the patient, then define the lesion":["u310"],"predicting-recurrence-and-preventing-the-next-bleed|Predicting recurrence and preventing the next bleed":["u311"],"unexplained-iron-or-vitamin-b12-deficiency|Unexplained iron or vitamin B12 deficiency":["u312"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u28"],"copy|Autoimmune atrophic gastritis can cause achlorhydria and pernicious anemia; diagnosis rests on corpus/fundus histology (p1335).":["u17"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u18"],"table-85-2-book-image|Table 85-2 — book image":["u19"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 85-2, p.1316":["u20","u33"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u313"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u34"],"copy|CHAPTER 85 / HAZZARD":["u23"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 85; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u36"],"copy|Presentation & diagnosis":["u25"],"copy|Acid suppression & H. pylori":["u26"],"copy|Upper GI bleeding":["u27"],"copy|For May 2024 Q73, trace each drug to its mechanism.":["u40"],"copy|Amlodipine is a calcium-channel blocker and can lower sphincter pressure, so gastric contents can still reflux during PPI treatment.":["u41"],"copy|Table 85-2 places nitrates, theophylline, benzodiazepines, dopaminergic agents, tricyclic antidepressants and anticholinergics in this same column.":["u42"],"copy|Aspirin, NSAIDs, potassium salts, ferrous sulfate, corticosteroids and alendronate appear in the direct mucosal-injury column.":["u43"],"copy|Ibuprofen, prednisone and aspirin can therefore matter clinically even though the question asks specifically about sphincter pressure (p1316).":["u44"],"copy|An older person with dysphagia, anemia or unintentional weight loss needs investigation even if heartburn is mild.":["u45"],"copy|Endoscopy assesses erosions, a stricture, Barrett esophagus or another mucosal lesion.":["u46"],"copy|A contrast study can delineate a ring, narrowing or impaired movement, and videofluoroscopy can help localize swallowing dysfunction.":["u47"],"copy|A response to empiric acid suppression cannot identify which lesion is present (p1317–1318).":["u48"],"copy|The book describes PPI-based clarithromycin triple therapy, including regimens as short as 7 days, and reports improved clearance with longer treatment (p1328).":["u49"],"copy|Treatment history and susceptibility matter when applying these older trial results.":["u50"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): In North America, when susceptibility is unknown, the ACG recommends 14 days of optimized bismuth quadruple therapy for treatment-naive patients.":["u51"],"copy|Clarithromycin-containing therapy requires demonstrated susceptibility.":["u52"],"copy|Confirm clearance at least four weeks after antibiotics, withholding the PPI for two weeks before testing. - American College of Gastroenterology, 2024.":["u53"],"copy|The book's higher hemoglobin threshold belongs to its discussion of stabilization.":["u54"],"copy|Active blood loss, perfusion and comorbid disease guide immediate assessment alongside the measured value (p1332–1333).":["u55"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): For hospitalized patients with overt upper gastrointestinal bleeding, the ACG suggests a restrictive red-cell transfusion threshold of 7 g/dL. - American College of Gastroenterology, 2021.":["u56"],"copy|Think of the esophagus as a tube that must both prevent reflux and clear material that reaches it.":["u57"],"copy|In Table 85-1, a short, weak lower esophageal sphincter and a hiatus hernia compromise the barrier.":["u58"],"copy|Effective peristalsis, upright posture and saliva help clear and buffer refluxate.":["u59"],"copy|A person with impaired mobility, reduced saliva or poor esophageal clearance has less protection even if gastric acid output is unremarkable.":["u60"],"copy|Bile-containing reflux and weaker mucosal repair can add to injury.":["u61"],"copy|Severe mucosal disease in later life therefore does not require unusually high acid production.":["u62"],"copy|Review anatomy, function, posture and medicines together (p1315–1316).":["u63"],"copy|Figures 85-1 and 85-2 describe different directions of association in a series of 840 people with endoscopically identified reflux esophagitis.":["u64"],"copy|Typical symptoms such as heartburn and acid regurgitation became less prominent with age, while dysphagia, anorexia, anemia and weight loss became more common.":["u65"],"copy|From the youngest to oldest groups, heartburn/reflux fell from 76% to 24%, while vomiting rose from 4% to 30%.":["u66"],"copy|Table 85-3 also lists chronic cough, hoarseness and noncardiac chest pain among atypical presentations (p1317–1318).":["u67"],"copy|These are observations in patients with established disease.":["u68"],"copy|A new loss of weight or swallowing difficulty cannot safely be diagnosed as reflux simply because both occurred in the study.":["u69"],"copy|Ask whether solids, liquids or both cause difficulty, whether intake has changed and whether anemia or bleeding accompanies the complaint (p1317–1318).":["u70"],"copy|A persistent symptom during high-dose PPI therapy does not answer the May 2024 Q73 mechanism question.":["u71"],"copy|Suppressing gastric acid changes the acidity of what refluxes; it does not directly restore sphincter pressure or prevent every episode of reflux.":["u72"],"copy|Amlodipine can allow more reflux by lowering that pressure.":["u73"],"copy|The patient's simultaneous NSAID, aspirin and steroid exposures matter because mucosal injury can compound the symptoms and raise concern about a different lesion.":["u74"],"copy|Review any continued indication for each drug and how tablets are taken.":["u75"],"copy|A pill that remains in contact with mucosa can injure it by a mechanism independent of reflux (p1316–1318).":["u76"],"copy|Endoscopy identifies erosions, ulcers and narrowing, permits biopsy, and can show Barrett esophagus or a concurrent gastric or duodenal lesion.":["u77"],"copy|Absence of erosions does not exclude reflux symptoms.":["u78"],"copy|A contrast examination can demonstrate a hiatus hernia, ring or stricture and can provide information on bolus passage.":["u79"],"copy|When the history points to oropharyngeal dysfunction, videofluoroscopic assessment may reveal a swallowing problem that acid suppression cannot treat.":["u80"],"copy|The decision to investigate should include whether alarm symptoms are present, the person's fitness for the procedure and what a result would change (p1317–1318).":["u81"],"copy|Figure 85-3 grades erosion by extent.":["u82"],"copy|In Los Angeles grade A, a break is no longer than 5 mm and does not extend between the tops of two mucosal folds.":["u83"],"copy|Grade B has a break longer than 5 mm without such bridging.":["u84"],"copy|In grade C the breaks extend between fold tops but involve less than 75% of the circumference; grade D involves at least 75%.":["u85"],"copy|The first boundary concerns length and the second concerns circumference.":["u86"],"copy|A patient able to eat despite mild heartburn can still have extensive erosive disease, and the original endoscopic grade matters when planning whether maintenance treatment is warranted (p1318, p1321–1322).":["u87"],"copy|The chapter treats a therapeutic PPI trial cautiously in older people because relief cannot distinguish esophagitis from other acid-responsive conditions and failure to improve does not identify the reason.":["u88"],"copy|Persistent symptoms after mucosal healing raise the possibilities of continued reflux, nonerosive reflux, altered motility or a different diagnosis.":["u89"],"copy|Check the drug and administration history, the initial endoscopic findings and whether the dominant symptom is actually dysphagia.":["u90"],"copy|In someone with a large hernia, aspiration or severe refractory stricture, anatomy may matter more to the next decision than empiric escalation of acid suppression (p1317–1318, p1321–1324).":["u91"],"copy|Keep four aims separate: relieve distress, heal mucosal injury, prevent a relapse and prevent a complication.":["u92"],"copy|Antacids or alginates may relieve mild symptoms without supplying the healing rates of a PPI in erosive disease.":["u93"],"copy|H2-receptor antagonists can be effective but generally heal fewer erosions.":["u94"],"copy|Trial comparisons summarized in the chapter evaluated omeprazole 20 mg daily, lansoprazole 30 mg daily, pantoprazole 40 mg daily and rabeprazole 20 mg daily.":["u95"],"copy|Figure 85-4 distinguishes empirical symptom response from response in endoscopy-negative disease: the PPI/placebo relative risks for persistent symptoms were 0.37 and 0.71, respectively (p1319).":["u96"],"copy|In Figure 85-5 the PPI/placebo estimate favors PPI for healing esophagitis (0.22 for persistence, compared with 0.74 for H2 blockade versus placebo).":["u97"],"copy|In the network comparison in Figure 85-6, esomeprazole 40 mg had higher healing rates than several standard-dose comparators at four weeks, with fewer differences at eight weeks.":["u98"],"copy|Do not convert a group difference in one endpoint into a claim that all older patients require the highest dose (p1318–1320).":["u99"],"copy|Read the dose key in Figure 85-9 as half / standard / double amounts: omeprazole 10 / 20 / 40 mg, pantoprazole 20 / 40 / 80 mg, lansoprazole 15 / 30 / 60 mg, rabeprazole 10 / 20 / 40 mg and esomeprazole 20 / 40 / 80 mg.":["u100"],"copy|The printed scheme uses a standard dose for 4–8 weeks for nonerosive disease and 8–12 weeks for Los Angeles A/B esophagitis, with double dosing over 8–12 weeks for C/D.":["u101"],"copy|Reduced or standard maintenance depends on the indication and response.":["u102"],"copy|Figure 85-19 uses the same dose key for gastric indications, including standard-dose treatment for eight weeks for NSAID-associated gastroduodenal lesions.":["u103"],"copy|In Figure 85-7, dexlansoprazole 60 mg was associated with more discontinuations than omeprazole 20 mg, pantoprazole 40 mg or lansoprazole 30 mg (p1319, p1322, p1331).":["u104"],"copy|Figure 85-9 separates healing therapy from maintenance and shows standard, reduced and higher doses.":["u105"],"copy|Table 85-4 identifies relapse factors such as a long history, severe baseline erosions, a large hiatus hernia and persistent symptoms even after healing.":["u106"],"copy|Figure 85-8 shows 12-month remission in 95% who continued maintenance versus 33% who stopped after six months (p1321).":["u107"],"copy|The chapter gives a 70% to 90% annual relapse rate after stopping an effective antisecretory regimen, an estimate about recurrence in reflux disease rather than a reason to continue treatment without an indication.":["u108"],"copy|A symptomatic patient with uncomplicated nonerosive disease and a patient with prior severe esophagitis, stricture or Barrett esophagus may reasonably reach different maintenance decisions (p1319, p1321–1322).":["u109"],"copy|Review the original indication, response, current risks and patient goals when renewing a long-term PPI.":["u110"],"copy|Observational studies cited in the chapter associate use with fractures and enteric infection, and the text discusses possible effects on mineral or vitamin absorption.":["u111"],"copy|Association does not quantify an individual patient's excess risk or establish that the drug caused a subsequent fracture.":["u112"],"copy|Conversely, the risk of recurrent ulcer bleeding during necessary antiplatelet or NSAID exposure can be substantial.":["u113"],"copy|In the earlier exam vignettes, prolonged esomeprazole exposure is the relevant association with C. difficile, and fracture risk is the recognized skeletal concern; malignant transformation of Barrett esophagus is not presented as an established PPI consequence (p1322–1324).":["u114"],"copy|Role of surgery depends on the problem and the patient's reserve.":["u115"],"copy|The chapter discusses referral for medical treatment failure, complications that cannot be controlled endoscopically, and substantial dysphagia or aspiration in association with a large hernia.":["u116"],"copy|A difficult symptom needs diagnostic clarification before an operation becomes the answer.":["u117"],"copy|Distinguish relief of reflux from evaluation of dysplasia in Barrett esophagus, which requires a decision specific to the mucosal finding.":["u118"],"copy|Comorbidity and the expertise of the treating center enter the balance for an older surgical candidate (p1324).":["u119"],"copy|Figure 85-10 diagrams possible mechanisms of long-term PPI adverse effects, including impaired nutrient absorption, altered gut flora, interstitial nephritis and a CYP2C19 interaction with clopidogrel.":["u120"],"copy|The diagram does not establish causation for each reported clinical association (p1323).":["u121"],"copy|An ulcer is a deeper defect than a superficial gastric erosion.":["u122"],"copy|In an older person, pain can be vague, mild or absent until bleeding, obstruction or perforation brings the patient to attention.":["u123"],"copy|Ask about early satiety, reduced intake, vomiting, melena and fatigue or dyspnea from blood loss.":["u125"],"copy|A long medication list and another illness that seems to account for fatigue can delay recognition.":["u126"],"copy|An NSAID or antithrombotic increases concern even if the patient reports no prior dyspepsia (p1325–1327).":["u127"],"copy|Figure 85-11 distinguishes esophageal, gastric and duodenal-bulb sites.":["u128"],"copy|In Figure 85-12, Finnish incidence of ulcers and severe complications rises steeply at older ages in both sexes and is higher in men (p1325).":["u129"],"copy|U.S. hospitalization rates in Figure 85-13 were lower in the later period but highest at age 65 or older; in Figure 85-14, mortality after upper-GI complications among those over 75 fell from 38% in 2001 to 31% in 2009 (p1326).":["u130"],"copy|H. pylori and mucosal injury from NSAIDs or aspirin are major causes and can coexist.":["u131"],"copy|Figure 85-16 compares NSAID/aspirin users with nonusers: gastric ulcers in 20% versus 7.5%, duodenal ulcers in 21% versus 12% (p1327).":["u133"],"copy|Prostaglandins contribute to mucosal defenses; inhibiting cyclooxygenase can undermine those defenses, while aspirin's platelet effect contributes to the consequences of bleeding.":["u134"],"copy|Enteric coating or the absence of prior pain does not establish a protected gastric mucosa.":["u135"],"copy|Establish which agent is used, dose and frequency, use of nonprescription analgesics, prior ulcer or bleed, and whether anti-inflammatory treatment remains necessary.":["u136"],"copy|A patient with a prior bleeding ulcer who must continue an NSAID has a different need for protection from someone with no ongoing exposure (p1325–1327, p1329–1331).":["u137"],"copy|Endoscopy localizes and characterizes the lesion, permits histology and helps identify H. pylori.":["u138"],"copy|Gastric ulcers may require biopsy to distinguish benign ulceration from malignancy.":["u139"],"copy|A radiographic study cannot replace tissue evaluation where cancer is a concern.":["u140"],"copy|If there has been recent bleeding or acid suppression, an initial negative H. pylori result can be falsely reassuring.":["u141"],"copy|Review which tests were performed, when antibiotics or a PPI were last taken and which gastric regions were sampled.":["u142"],"copy|The chance of missing patchy infection can increase in atrophic mucosa or when sampling is limited to one region (p1326–1328).":["u143"],"copy|Table 85-5 compares tests by the question each answers.":["u144"],"copy|Histology provides information on inflammation and atrophy as well as the organism.":["u145"],"copy|The rapid urease test is quick but can miss infection after PPI, antibiotic or bismuth exposure and during acute bleeding.":["u146"],"copy|Culture can allow antibiotic susceptibility testing, although laboratory availability and technical requirements limit routine use.":["u147"],"copy|A positive antibody result does not distinguish remote exposure from persistent active infection and is unsuitable as proof of cure.":["u148"],"copy|Breath testing and fecal antigen testing can address active infection without requiring another endoscopy when there is no separate reason for direct examination (p1327–1328).":["u149"],"copy|The historical regimens in the book combine a PPI twice daily with clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily and either amoxicillin 1 g twice daily or a nitroimidazole 500 mg twice daily for at least 7 days.":["u150"],"copy|It reviews short-course trials and notes better eradication with longer treatment.":["u151"],"copy|Older participants in these studies were selected under particular resistance patterns; their results do not predict local effectiveness when clarithromycin resistance is common.":["u152"],"copy|The current ACG note above specifies the contemporary empiric alternative.":["u153"],"copy|Confirm eradication after the relevant medication-free interval rather than treating improvement in pain or restoration of appetite as microbiological proof (p1327–1329).":["u154"],"copy|Table 85-6 summarizes historical trials in older patients.":["u155"],"copy|Clarithromycin 250 mg twice daily and 500 mg twice daily showed no significant difference in cure or tolerability in the studies reviewed.":["u156"],"copy|Other components were amoxicillin 1 g twice daily or 500 mg three times daily, metronidazole 250 mg four times daily or 500 mg twice daily, and tinidazole 500 mg twice daily.":["u157"],"copy|The sequential course used PPI plus amoxicillin for 5 days, then PPI plus clarithromycin and tinidazole for another 5 days.":["u158"],"copy|The quadruple course combined PPI, tetracycline 500 mg (frequency unspecified in the footnote), metronidazole and bismuth subcitrate 240 mg twice daily for 10 days.":["u159"],"copy|Study PPI schedules included omeprazole or esomeprazole 20 mg once or twice daily, lansoprazole 30 mg twice daily, pantoprazole 40 mg daily and rabeprazole 20 mg twice daily (p1328–1329).":["u160"],"copy|Read the eradication columns as intention-to-treat followed by per-protocol.":["u161"],"copy|PPI plus clarithromycin and a nitroimidazole achieved 88%/91%; two amoxicillin/clarithromycin rows give 84%/89% and 80%/83%; amoxicillin/metronidazole gives 80%/84%.":["u162"],"copy|The sequential regimen achieved 94%/97%, the quadruple regimen 91%/95%.":["u163"],"copy|Adverse effects occurred in 10% and 28%, respectively, in those latter rows.":["u164"],"copy|These are historical pooled regimens under the susceptibility conditions studied.":["u165"],"copy|The printed trial doses do not replace the separately cited contemporary empiric regimen (p1329).":["u166"],"copy|For prevention of NSAID-associated severe mucosal injury, the book compares PPIs and misoprostol with H2 antagonists.":["u167"],"copy|Misoprostol 200 micrograms four times daily was effective, but diarrhea and other adverse effects were more frequent than with PPIs.":["u168"],"copy|Prior ulcer, continued NSAID exposure and concomitant antiplatelet therapy affect the benefit of protection (p1329–1331).":["u169"],"copy|When drug-associated ulcers are suspected, withdrawing the causative agent if possible and providing an effective healing regimen address different parts of the problem.":["u170"],"copy|Review aspirin's indication separately from an analgesic NSAID.":["u171"],"copy|Eradicating H. pylori can reduce recurrent ulcer risk in appropriate patients but does not remove the continuing hazard of ulcerogenic drugs.":["u172"],"copy|Figure 85-17 adds frailty, disability, prior ulcer or bleeding and co-medications to NSAID selection, gastroprotection and H. pylori assessment.":["u173"],"copy|An apparently straightforward deprescribing decision can be unsafe if it removes gastroprotection while the patient continues a medicine that previously contributed to bleeding (p1329–1331, p1334).":["u175"],"copy|What if the ulcer persists after initial therapy?":["u176"],"copy|First distinguish inadequate treatment from an incorrect causal label.":["u177"],"copy|Confirm the patient took the intended dose, excluded nonprescription NSAIDs and completed any prescribed eradication regimen.":["u178"],"copy|Consider whether a negative H. pylori result was obtained during bleeding or while acid suppression or antibiotics reduced sensitivity.":["u179"],"copy|For an uncomplicated ulcer classified as neither NSAID-related nor H. pylori-related, the book describes a standard-dose PPI for at least 4 weeks for a duodenal ulcer and 8 weeks for a gastric ulcer.":["u180"],"copy|Persistent or atypical disease warrants further assessment, including a possible hypersecretory state or ongoing damaging medication, rather than unexamined continuation of the same treatment (p1327–1328, p1330).":["u181"],"copy|When reading eradication trials, distinguish the chance of cure among all participants starting a regimen from the chance among people who completed it as planned.":["u182"],"copy|Table 85-6 reports both intention-to-treat and per-protocol results, alongside treatment withdrawals and adverse effects.":["u183"],"copy|The difference matters in an older person facing multiple daily medicines, nausea or difficulty following an eradication schedule.":["u184"],"copy|A regimen with appealing efficacy among completers may be a poor choice if interactions or intolerance are likely to interrupt treatment.":["u185"],"copy|Assess the feasibility of the course at prescribing, and determine success with a properly timed test for active infection (p1328–1329).":["u186"],"copy|The anatomic definition of upper GI bleeding is a source proximal to the ligament of Treitz.":["u187"],"copy|Hematemesis, blood or coffee-ground material in gastric contents and melena suggest this region, whereas hematochezia can occur with brisk upper-tract hemorrhage.":["u188"],"copy|Pallor, dyspnea, angina, presyncope, falls or decompensated cardiac disease may be more prominent in an older person than a complaint of bleeding.":["u189"],"copy|Assess perfusion, vital signs and mental status, establish venous access, and consider the effects of ongoing losses and comorbid disease.":["u190"],"copy|The decision to resuscitate should not wait for a hemoglobin concentration to fall to its eventual nadir (p1331–1333).":["u191"],"copy|Peptic ulcers are frequent sources, but Table 85-7 includes erosive lesions, varices, tumors and tears.":["u192"],"copy|Portal hypertension makes varices possible but does not prove the site.":["u193"],"copy|A patient with cirrhosis who also takes an NSAID can bleed from an ulcer.":["u194"],"copy|Initial history should include prior endoscopy, liver disease, prior bleeding, all antithrombotic and anti-inflammatory drugs and any recent change in dose or kidney function.":["u195"],"copy|A test identifying an anticoagulant effect helps explain a tendency to bleed; it does not replace finding the lesion.":["u196"],"copy|Support circulation and arrange timely endoscopy in light of stability and suspected source (p1331–1333).":["u197"],"copy|The book associates upper-GI bleeding more often with dabigatran 150 mg twice daily, rivaroxaban, or edoxaban 60 mg daily than with warfarin in the comparisons cited.":["u198"],"copy|It specifies no rivaroxaban dose in this passage.":["u199"],"copy|Risk also depends on renal impairment, previous upper-GI bleeding, H. pylori and concurrent ulcerogenic drugs; the comparison is not a universal ranking for every anticoagulant dose or patient (p1332).":["u200"],"copy|Endoscopy provides diagnosis, predicts risk and permits treatment.":["u201"],"copy|In Figure 85-20, the Forrest system pairs appearance with rebleeding risk: 55% for active hemorrhage, 43% for a nonbleeding visible vessel, 22% for an adherent clot, 10% for a flat spot and 5% for a clean base in the series shown (p1333).":["u202"],"copy|Ia means spurting and Ib oozing hemorrhage, IIa a visible vessel without active bleeding, IIb an adherent clot, IIc a flat pigmented area and III a clean base.":["u203"],"copy|Active bleeding and a nonbleeding visible vessel carry important risk and are candidates for endoscopic hemostasis.":["u204"],"copy|A clean ulcer base has a different risk from a visible vessel despite the absence of active bleeding in both.":["u205"],"copy|The purpose of the pre-endoscopy high-dose PPI regimen is distinct from treatment after successful hemostasis of high-risk stigmata.":["u206"],"copy|Table 85-8 reports less rebleeding and less surgery for endoscopic treatment plus PPI compared with PPI alone; the mortality comparison was not significant.":["u207"],"copy|Acid suppression does not replace endoscopic control of a lesion that continues to bleed (p1333–1334).":["u208"],"copy|Assess suspected variceal hemorrhage on its own pathway.":["u209"],"copy|The chapter describes early use of a vasoactive agent such as somatostatin, octreotide or terlipressin when variceal bleeding is suspected.":["u210"],"copy|Endoscopic band ligation and management of underlying portal hypertension address a different mechanism from clipping an ulcer vessel.":["u211"],"copy|In a patient at high risk of liver failure or recurrent hemorrhage, a portosystemic shunt may be considered in an appropriate specialist setting.":["u212"],"copy|For recurrent variceal hemorrhage, beta-blockers and repeat ligation have roles, but hypotension, fatigue and orthostatic effects may constrain beta-blockade in a frail older person (p1333–1335).":["u213"],"copy|Table 85-9 compares the pre-endoscopic Glasgow-Blatchford score (0–23), clinical/endoscopic Rockall score (0–11) and eight-domain frailty-based Multidimensional Prognostic Index (0–1) (p1335).":["u214"],"copy|The Glasgow-Blatchford assessment uses pre-endoscopic information such as blood urea, hemoglobin, pressure, pulse, melena, syncope and relevant liver or cardiac disease to estimate the likelihood of needing intervention.":["u215"],"copy|The book identifies a score of 0 as low risk.":["u216"],"copy|Rockall adds endoscopic diagnosis and stigmata to its clinical assessment and focuses on recurrence and mortality.":["u217"],"copy|The multidimensional prognostic index addresses a different question: functional status, nutrition, cognition, disease burden, medication burden and social circumstances can influence the eventual outcome even after a bleeding lesion is controlled.":["u218"],"copy|A favorable endoscopic appearance does not eliminate the need to assess what help the patient will require after discharge (p1334–1335).":["u219"],"copy|For secondary prevention, ask why aspirin was prescribed.":["u220"],"copy|The book advises early resumption after bleeding stops when established cardiovascular disease makes it important, ideally in 1–3 days and within 7 days, alongside long-term PPI protection.":["u221"],"copy|It generally argues against restarting aspirin used solely for primary prevention in most such patients.":["u222"],"copy|Continued NSAID use should be reconsidered; where unavoidable the chapter describes a selective COX-2 inhibitor at the lowest effective dose with daily PPI.":["u223"],"copy|Confirm H. pylori clearance and review co-prescribed antithrombotics.":["u224"],"copy|Preventing another bleed and preventing a vascular event are decisions to be made together (p1334).":["u225"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): For cardiac aspirin used for secondary prevention during acute gastrointestinal bleeding, ACG/CAG suggest continuing it.":["u226"],"copy|If interrupted, resume on the day that hemostasis is confirmed endoscopically. - ACG/CAG antithrombotic guideline, 2022.":["u227"],"copy|Autoimmune atrophic gastritis can remain clinically quiet for years.":["u228"],"copy|The pathological process affects the corpus and fundus, replacing functioning gastric mucosa with atrophic or metaplastic tissue.":["u229"],"copy|Parietal-cell injury reduces acid secretion; antibodies to intrinsic factor compromise vitamin B12 absorption.":["u230"],"copy|Refractory iron deficiency can also bring the patient to attention.":["u231"],"copy|A person with early satiety or reflux-like symptoms may be producing little acid, making indefinite empiric acid suppression a poor substitute for explaining the symptoms.":["u232"],"copy|Gastrin and pepsinogen measurements can support assessment, but the chapter bases the diagnosis on appropriately directed endoscopic biopsies and emphasizes excluding associated neoplasia (p1335).":["u233"],"co